2026-10-10 09:23:30,584 INFO 💭 THOUGHT (primary)
DISCUSSION
This confirms it: choose any point, get Q = dG, submit any d' with d'G = Q. So the whole task is to provide a point of small known order. Because both listed curves are prime-order groups, the only way is to exploit the fact that not every affine point on the Weierstrass equation has prime order if the curve order isn't actually prime over the full group generated by arbitrary points—but here it is. So I should compute the exact group order from the given `n`; if `n` is the total curve order, then every non-infinity point indeed has prime order and there's no issue unless there's an input-validation bug. A classic bug is allowing negative hex strings or huge integers parsed to mpz then reduced by the curve equation; but they also require `0 <= x,y < p`, killing that. The remaining option is that `random.randint(1, n)` can return `n`, so Q = nG = O for any valid G. If we can retry until Q is Origin, then any multiple of ord(G) works; but with prime order that's probability 1/n, impossible. However if we choose G = O somehow, then Q is always O and any d verifies. Since `recv_generator` constructs `Point(Gx,Gy)`, we cannot literally send O. I want to probe whether some affine point causes multiplication to return the string `Origin` for all d due to implementation quirks. A good target is a point with y=0 if it exists on P-256 or secp256k1, since then 2P = O mathematically. I'll search properly for roots of x^3+ax+b mod p on both curves.

